beginning in 2013 women from all over  the world left their homes to join Isis  thousands of these women remain in Syria  and Iraq where they continue to  fundraise and spread the group's  ideology but women also played prominent  roles in combating Isis from Jordanian  fighter pilots to Kurdish infantry units  women were on the front line we  shouldn't be surprised women formed a  significant minority of fighters in some  of the world's longest-running conflicts  including the civil wars in Colombia and  Sri Lanka women have served in many  roles and armed groups maintaining camps  and serving as medics and fighters while  many women take up arms for the same  reasons as men my research finds that  women are more likely to fight when  their homes families and safety are at  stake an armed groups ideology also  matters women are more likely to join  groups that fight to protect and expand  their rights the rise and fall of Isis  demonstrates that whatever woman's  motivations are their participation is  now a fundamental feature of war does  women's growing participation in armed  groups complicate the nature of war let  us know in our poll also let us know  what other topics you'd like our  scholars to cover in 60 seconds and be  sure to LIKE and subscribe for more  research and videos from AEI
 

